= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take a complete, right-continuous <filtration> $(\mathcal F_t)$, an increasing family of sigma-algebras. Adapted means that $X_t$ is $\mathcal F_t$-measurable at each $t$; <càdlàg> means right-continuous with left limits. A <local martingale> is an adapted <càdlàg> process $X$, with integrable initial value, for which there are <stopping times> $\tau_n\uparrow\infty$ almost surely such that each <stopped process> $X^{\tau_n}_t=X_{t\wedge\tau_n}$ is a true <martingale>. Such a sequence is a localizing sequence. A true <martingale> is integrable at each time and satisfies $\mathbb E(X_t\mid\mathcal F_s)=X_s$ for $s\le t$.

The necessary and sufficient condition is that $X$ is a <class DL process>:
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is a martingale}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \{X_\tau:\tau\le T\text{ a stopping time}\}\text{ is uniformly integrable for every finite }T.}
$$
A family $\mathcal Z$ is <uniformly integrable> if its members are integrable and $\sup_{Z\in\mathcal Z}\mathbb E(|Z|\mathbf1_{\{|Z|>K\}})\to0$ as $K\to\infty$. A <stopping time> $\tau$ satisfies $\{\tau\le t\}\in\mathcal F_t$ for all $t$.

For necessity, on $[0,T]$ the <optional stopping theorem> gives $X_\tau=\mathbb E(X_T\mid\mathcal F_\tau)$; <conditional expectations> of one integrable variable form a <uniformly integrable> family. For sufficiency, $X_{t\wedge\tau_n}\to X_t$ almost surely, and <class DL> upgrades this convergence to $L^1$ on each finite horizon. Pass to the limit in $\mathbb E(X_{t\wedge\tau_n}\mid\mathcal F_s)=X_{s\wedge\tau_n}$ to obtain the <martingale> identity. Requiring <uniform integrability> of all times on the entire half-line would be stronger than necessary; <Brownian motion> is already a counterexample to that stronger requirement.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Lévy characterization of multidimensional Brownian motion> says that a continuous adapted $\mathbb R^d$-valued process $Z$, with $Z_0=0$, is standard <Brownian motion> relative to the given <filtration> if and only if its coordinates are <local martingales> and
$$
\boxed{[Z^i,Z^j]_t=\delta_{ij}t\qquad(1\le i,j\le d).}
$$
For <Brownian motion>, independent centered Gaussian increments give the <martingale> property of each coordinate and of $Z_t^iZ_t^j-\delta_{ij}t$. The defining uniqueness of <quadratic covariation> consequently gives the bracket condition.

Conversely, suppose the <local martingale> and bracket conditions hold. For $\theta\in\mathbb R^d$, the <Itô formula> gives
$$
E_t=\exp\left(i\theta\cdot Z_t+\tfrac12|\theta|^2t\right),\qquad dE_t=iE_t\,\theta\cdot dZ_t,
$$
since the bracket term cancels the time derivative. On $[0,T]$, $|E_t|\le e^{|\theta|^2T/2}$, so its real and imaginary parts are bounded <local martingales> and hence true <martingales>. Thus, for $s\le t$,
$$
\mathbb E\left[e^{i\theta\cdot(Z_t-Z_s)}\mid\mathcal F_s\right]=e^{-\frac12|\theta|^2(t-s)}.
$$
This is the <characteristic function> of $N(0,(t-s)I_d)$ and is independent of $\mathcal F_s$. Uniqueness of <characteristic functions> shows that $Z_t-Z_s$ has that <Gaussian distribution> and is independent of the past. Iterating over ordered times gives independent Gaussian increments; together with continuous paths and $Z_0=0$, these are precisely the defining properties of standard <Brownian motion>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First justify that the logarithm is defined at all times from one onward. For planar <Brownian motion> started at $z\ne0$, stop on exiting the annulus $\varepsilon<|x|<R$, where $\varepsilon<|z|<R$. This exit time $\rho$ is finite almost surely: the stopped identity $|B_t|^2-2t$ gives $\mathbb E(t\wedge\rho)\le R^2/2$. The function $f(x)=\log|x|$ is harmonic on the annulus, so its <stopped process> is a bounded <martingale>. Optional stopping gives
$$
\mathbb P_z(\text{hit }\varepsilon\text{ before }R)=\frac{\log R-\log|z|}{\log R-\log\varepsilon}\longrightarrow0.
$$
Hitting zero before the outer circle would require hitting every inner circle first. It therefore has probability zero. Taking a countable sequence of outer radii tending to infinity proves that <planar Brownian motion avoids a fixed point>. Since $B_1$ has a density and is nonzero almost surely, condition on $\mathcal F_1$ to apply this result to the future path.

For $x\ne0$, $\nabla f(x)=x/|x|^2$ and $\Delta f(x)=0$ in two dimensions. The <Itô formula> on annuli therefore gives
$$
\boxed{X_t=X_1+\int_1^t\frac{B_s}{|B_s|^2}\cdot dB_s\qquad(t\ge1).}
$$
To localize explicitly, let $\tau_n=\inf\{t\ge1:|B_t|\notin(1/n,n)\}$. These <stopping times> increase to infinity almost surely: on each compact time interval the continuous path has a finite maximum and, by point avoidance, a positive minimum radius. Up to $\tau_n$, the stochastic integrand has norm at most $n$. Its stopped integral is square-integrable on every finite horizon. The initial value $\log|B_1|$ is integrable, as follows from the Rayleigh-density calculation in part d. Hence $X^{\tau_n}$ is a true <martingale> from time one and **$X$ is a <local martingale>**. This also gives its <quadratic variation> from time one as $\int_1^t|B_s|^{-2}\,ds$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<Brownian scaling> gives $|B_t|\overset{d}=\sqrt t\,R$, where $R=|B_1|$ has <Rayleigh distribution> with density $r e^{-r^2/2}$, $r>0$. For every $p>0$,
$$
\mathbb E|\log R|^p=\int_0^\infty|\log r|^p r e^{-r^2/2}\,dr<\infty.
$$
Near zero this is bounded by $\int_0^1|\log r|^p r\,dr=\int_0^\infty u^pe^{-2u}\,du$, which is finite; near infinity the Gaussian tail dominates any logarithmic power. The inequality $|a+b|^p\le C_p(|a|^p+|b|^p)$, valid also for $0<p<1$, now yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E|X_t|^p<\infty\qquad(t\ge1,\ p>0).}
$$
Let $c=\mathbb E\log R$, a finite constant. The same scaling identity gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E X_t=\tfrac12\log t+c\longrightarrow\infty.}
$$
An integrable <martingale> has constant expectation, so the <logarithm of planar Brownian radius> is a <strict local martingale> on $[1,\infty)$. Fixed-time integrability, even of every positive power, does not imply the stopping-time <uniform integrability> required in part a.

